From: Maria Delgado
To: All
Subj: Hard card or real hard disk?
Date: 08-09-88 16:30

Customers keep asking me this at the store, so let's settle it here.

A hard card is a hard disk and its controller on one expansion card.
You plug it into a slot and you're done: no drive bay, no cables, no
screwdriver.  A regular hard disk goes in a drive bay with a separate
controller card and two ribbon cables.

My take: the hard card is wonderful if your case is full or your hands
are shaky.  The regular drive is cheaper per megabyte and easier to
upgrade.  Either one beats swapping floppies like a blackjack dealer.

Maria
@@4
From: Karen Whitfield
To: Maria Delgado
Subj: Re: Hard card or real hard disk?
Date: 08-10-88 06:55

> Either one beats swapping floppies like a blackjack dealer.

Agreed.  Two things to add:

1. A hard card is heavy and it hangs off a slot.  If you move the
   machine a lot (hello, portable owners), screw the bracket in tight.
2. Park your heads before you move ANY hard disk, unless the drive
   parks itself.  Most new ones do.  The old ones don't, and they will
   let you know about it exactly once.

KW
