Date: 11-14-89 19:50

I'm helping my neighbour set up his new ARM/AT (I'm allowed to touch
other people's computers, I just won't own one).  On the front panel
there's POWER, TURBO, DISK, and an amber lamp that says X86.

It has never once lit up.  The manual says nothing.  The neighbour says
it's "for decoration".  I say nobody puts a lamp on a computer for
decoration.  Well, except Europa Micro, apparently.

What does it do?
@@13
From: Karen Whitfield
To: Rick Lambert
Subj: Re: What is the X86 lamp for?
Date: 11-15-89 06:40

> It has never once lit up.

It lights while the machine is running x86 code.

ARM-DOS comes with ELBOW, a program that runs genuine old x86 DOS
programs on the ARM, instruction by instruction, translating as it
goes.  The lamp is the machine telling you it's doing an impression.

Try it: at the DOS prompt, type

    CD \ELBOW
    DEMO

and watch the front panel.  Or run one of the samples in C:\ELBOW with
X86 in front of the name, like   X86 C:\ELBOW\FIRE.COM   and keep an eye
on the lamp while the fire burns.

KW
