@@17
From: Dave Morgan
To: All
Subj: What is extended memory FOR?
Date: 02-08-90 21:15

MEM says I have 640K of conventional memory and 15 megabytes of
"extended memory".  Fifteen MEGABYTES.  Lotus uses the 640K and
complains.  What is the other fifteen megabytes doing?  Watching?
@@18
From: Karen Whitfield
To: Dave Morgan
Subj: Re: What is extended memory FOR?
Date: 02-08-90 23:05

> What is the other fifteen megabytes doing?  Watching?

Mostly, yes.  DOS programs live in the first 640K, because that's the
deal DOS made in 1981 and we're all still honouring it.  Memory above
1 MB is "extended memory", and a program has to ask for it, through
HIMEM.SYS (the XMS driver), to use it.

Who asks: disk caches, RAM disks, and the big new programs that know
how.  Lotus 2 doesn't.  So for you it's a very expensive audience.

Try MEM /PROGRAM for the 640K side of the story.  It lists every
program and driver sitting in memory, and how much each one ate.

KW
